From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 17:05:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733-cpu: Park the core clusters on the backup
 PLL

While a core cluster's PLL relocks, its cores run from the 26 MHz
oscillator. The clock framework's work around the change runs on those
cores too, so every rate change takes longer than the relock itself.

Park the core clusters on the backup PLL CPU_BACK instead, the clusters'
mux encoding 101, which the manual names as one of their backup sources
(5.1.4.1). It is used when the firmware runs it, enable and output gate
set, at no more than 1014 MHz. The driver does not enable it: the manual
advises against switching PLLs on and off during operation (5.1.4.2).
probe() makes the choice once and says which source it took.

The park must not run the cores faster than their supply allows. 1014
MHz is an operating point that both clusters run at the lowest voltage
of their supplies, so a park at or below it is safe in both directions
of a change, whatever the old and the new rate.

Not covered: the DSU, whose mux has no input from the backup PLL.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on two boards; probe reports the park
on the backup PLL that the boot firmware runs, and after rate changes in
both directions over the operating points the cores run at the set rate,
which the run time of a fixed loop shows on both clusters.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c
index 7a78ee2979b5..9a1e88cfdff3 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c
@@ -101,6 +101,7 @@
 
 /* Mux indices are the encodings */
 #define CPU_MUX_SLOW		0
+#define CPU_CORE_MUX_BACK	5
 
 /*
  * Operating points that the core clusters and the DSU run at the lowest
@@ -759,6 +760,30 @@ static int sun60i_a733_cpu_register_clks(struct device *dev, void __iomem *reg)
 	return sun60i_a733_cpu_set_rate_ranges();
 }
 
+/*
+ * Park the core clusters on the backup PLL instead of cpu-slow when the
+ * firmware runs it (enable and output gate set) at no more than
+ * CPU_CORE_LOW_VOLTAGE_RATE, so a park never runs the cores faster than
+ * their supply allows, in either direction of a change. The driver does
+ * not enable it: the manual advises against switching PLLs on and off
+ * during operation (5.1.4.2).
+ */
+static void sun60i_a733_cpu_park_on_back(struct device *dev)
+{
+	bool on = clk_hw_is_enabled(&pll_cpu_back_clk.hw);
+	unsigned long rate = clk_hw_get_rate(&pll_cpu_back_clk.hw);
+
+	if (!on || !rate || rate > CPU_CORE_LOW_VOLTAGE_RATE) {
+		dev_info(dev, "backup PLL %s at %lu Hz, core clusters park on cpu-slow\n",
+			 on ? "on" : "off", rate);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	cpu_l_mux_nb.bypass_index = CPU_CORE_MUX_BACK;
+	cpu_b_mux_nb.bypass_index = CPU_CORE_MUX_BACK;
+	dev_info(dev, "core clusters park on the backup PLL at %lu Hz\n", rate);
+}
+
 static int sun60i_a733_cpu_ccu_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -783,6 +808,8 @@ static int sun60i_a733_cpu_ccu_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "cannot register the clocks\n");
 
+	sun60i_a733_cpu_park_on_back(dev);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_a733_cpu_clusters); i++) {
 		ret = sun60i_a733_cpu_register_nb(dev,
 						  &sun60i_a733_cpu_clusters[i]);
